The toolchain, in one directory.

    cc          the C compiler this repository builds with: clang
    cxx         the C++ compiler: clang++, plus one flag (below)
    gcc         a shim onto ./cc,  for the one step that names gcc outright
    g++         a shim onto ./cxx, likewise
    compiler.sh WHICH clang, chosen rather than inherited -- sourced by both
    env.sh      sourced by embed/build.sh and every modules/*/build.sh

WHICH clang, AND WHY IT IS WRITTEN DOWN. `clang' as a bare name is
whatever PATH answers first, and on a Mac with Homebrew's LLVM installed
that is Homebrew's: /usr/local/opt/llvm/bin sits ahead of /usr/bin in an
ordinary login PATH. So this repository's compiler changed the day
somebody ran `brew install llvm' and would change back under a PATH that
did not have it -- a fact nobody wrote down and nobody could see. ./compiler.sh
makes the choice instead: Homebrew's LLVM where it is installed
($HOMEBREW_PREFIX, then /opt/homebrew, then /usr/local), the platform's
clang where it is not, and $CICILI_CC / $CICILI_CXX over both.

IT IS THE NEWER COMPILER AND IT SAYS MORE. Homebrew's 23.1.0 against
Apple's 21.0.0 here, and its libc++ headers have had the transitive
includes removed -- so code that reached a name through a header it never
asked for fails HERE rather than on somebody else's machine later.
ZiguratIP's Core/utility.hpp calling std::back_inserter with only
<algorithm> included is exactly that, and Homebrew's clang is what found
it. Apple's compiles it, which is not the same as it being right.

BUT ONE libc++ IN THE PROCESS, AND IT IS THE SYSTEM'S. Homebrew's clang
compiles against its own headers and still links /usr/lib/libc++.1.dylib,
which is what ZiguratIP's libCore, every module .so and the binary itself
already link -- checked with `otool -L', not assumed. Nothing here adds
-L/usr/local/opt/llvm/lib/c++ or an rpath to it, and nothing should: two
libc++ runtimes in one address space is the hazard the last paragraph of
this file is about, and cocolog links libCore into its own binary and
dlopen's modules into its own process.

GOING BACK TO APPLE'S: `make CICILI_CC=/usr/bin/clang CICILI_CXX=/usr/bin/clang++'.

WHY cxx IS A SCRIPT AND NOT A SYMLINK. clang has no C++ standard library
of its own on Linux: it borrows libstdc++ from a gcc installation, and it
picks the NEWEST one it can find. On Ubuntu 24.04 that is
/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-linux-gnu/14 -- a runtime directory shipped by
libgcc-14, holding crtbegin.o and libgcc_s.so and NOT ONE C++ HEADER,
because g++ itself is 13.3 and the headers are under /usr/include/c++/13.
Every C++ file then fails with

    fatal error: 'string' file not found

naming a header that is installed and readable. ./cxx walks the gcc
install dirs newest-first, takes the first that has a matching header set,
and passes --gcc-install-dir. It is COMPUTED rather than written down: a
hardcoded 13 is a build that breaks on the next machine.

WHY gcc AND g++ EXIST AT ALL. Cicili's config.lisp names the compiler
outright --

    'compiler `("libtool" "--tag=CC" "--mode=compile" "gcc" ,@opt ,*verbose*)

-- on Linux, and clang on Darwin. There is no environment override (the
{$PYTHON} and {$LIBTORCH} discovery reads getenv; the toolchain does not),
and a target's own :compile list is appended AFTER the base, so it can add
flags but cannot rename the program. cocolog does not patch Cicili -- it
is an INPUT to this repository and treats itself as frozen -- so the build
puts this directory on PATH for that step instead.

THE PATCH CICILI WOULD WANT, offered rather than applied:

    (let* ((os  (software-type))
           (cc  (or (uiop:getenv "CICILI_CC")  (if (string= os "Darwin") "clang"   "gcc")))
           (cxx (or (uiop:getenv "CICILI_CXX") (if (string= os "Darwin") "clang++" "g++")))
           ...)

and then `,cc' / `,cxx' where the strings are now. Two of the five files
here go away with it.

GOING BACK TO GCC: `make CICILI_CC=gcc CICILI_CXX=g++', and the same two
in the environment for the build scripts. Nothing here is load-bearing on
clang. What IS load-bearing is that all of it agrees: cocolog links
ZiguratIP's libCore into its own binary and dlopen's modules into its own
process, so the compiler is not a per-repository choice. ZiguratIP and The
Coco keep an identical copy of this directory, which is the honest cost of
three repositories that refuse to vendor each other.
